WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:00.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when we think about wildfires we often  see images of singed animals  and burning buildings fires add harmful  smoke into the air  and emit greenhouse gases they can  destroy homes  and lives but they aren't all bad  fires keep countless ecosystems healthy  they clear out dead brush that blocks  new plant growth  they add nutrients back into the soil  many plants flourish after a fire  lodgepole pines make some pine cones  that are covered in a layer of wax  it protects the seeds until a fire comes  through once the pine cones are exposed  to hot temperatures  the wax melts and the seeds are free  [Music]  ecosystems are used to a certain fire  regime which is a pattern of wildfire  some places need more some places need  less  for example tropical rainforests with  thin bark trees  aren't adapted to a lot of fire it can  quickly burn through the bark  and kill the trees on the other hand  many grasslands need regular fires that  keep larger bushes and trees from taking  hold  otherwise they may turn into woodlands  squeezing out the animals that used to  live there  because of human activity places around  the world are going through rapid  changes to their fire regimes  as people convert grassland to farmland  and move closer to wildfire prone areas  they've stopped natural fires from  burning this has happened in places  including brazil  tanzania and the eurasian steppe  slash and burn agriculture is causing  more fires in the amazon and indonesian  tropical forests  meanwhile climate change is lengthening  fire seasons in places used to fire  these devastating blazes are hard to  recover from  so how can we approach fire in a way  that balances human health  and recognizes it as a natural process  that promotes biodiversity  it requires a multifaceted approach for  one  we can let natural wildfires burn in  some areas  decades of fire suppression have led to  a build-up of dry logs and leaves  this can cause out-of-control blazes  places like yellowstone national park in  the united states are allowing small  natural fires  these make a patchwork of burned and  unburned areas  that increases the kinds of plants and  animals that can thrive there  when fires do rage we can protect areas  that may not be able to survive  during the 2019-2020 australian fires  firefighters worked to save a growth of  prehistoric trees called willow my pines  fewer than 200 of these trees exist in  the wild  firefighters set up irrigation to keep  the grove wet  and surrounded it with fire retardant  they managed to save the trees  we can also try to improve the health of  each ecosystem  one way is to reintroduce and protect  animals that are important to their  habitats  in south africa researchers found that  fires were 50 times smaller  when rhinos grazed the land the rhinos  kept plants from growing out of control  their paths created fire breaks or gaps  in vegetation  large grazers like the white rhino can  rebalance ecosystems and reset their  fire regimes  finally we can lean on ages old fire  approaches  in china people have planted green fire  breaks to manage wildfires for over a  thousand years  these sections don't burn as easily as  their surroundings  recently the chinese government planted  more than 364 thousand kilometers of  green fire breaks  indigenous australians have lit fires in  the bush for fifty thousand years  to hunt clear paths and invigorate the  land  areas without these fires have larger  and more dangerous blazes that are  triggered by lightning  using indigenous strategies for the land  can help manage fires in a sustainable  way  challenges are ahead but an integrated  approach to fire  may help us and the planet  [Music]  you

